---
name: tack
description: Plan and inspect switches of a stateful workload's primary between sites with the tack CLI, and read SiteFailover ledgers. Use when asked about a Tack run, a site switchover or failover, or a SiteFailover. Never start or resume a run without an explicit instruction from a human for that run.
---

# tack

Switch a stateful workload's primary between sites, with GitOps kept in step.

```text
Tack switches a stateful workload's primary from one site to another: a
planned switchover, or a failover after a site is lost. It acts through the
workload's own API, keeps GitOps in step, and records every step in a
SiteFailover ledger in the new primary's cluster.

A run takes four phases, each step recorded before it starts and after it ends:

  Hold             annotate only the objects the run changes with
                   kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/reconcile: disabled, so Flux
                   stops applying them
  Failover         change the live objects through the workload's API, in
                   order, waiting for each condition
  PromoteToGitOps  commit to Git the values that are now live
  Release          check that Git equals live, remove the annotations, and
                   check that Flux changed nothing

The operator starts every run. Tack never decides to fail over by itself.

Rules for AI agents:
  - An agent never starts "tack run" or "tack resume" without an explicit
    instruction from a human for that run.
  - "tack plan" comes first. Show its output to the human before any run.
  - An agent never passes --yes, --fence, --accept-git-head, --accept-behind,
    --take-over or --force unless the human's instruction for that run names
    it.
  - "tack plan" and "tack status" only read; an agent may run them freely.
  - When a run stops, report its reason and message to the human and wait.

Exit codes:
  0  success
  1  error
  2  usage error
  3  the run stopped and waits for the operator (see tack status)
  4  the run waits for something outside it, such as Git (tack resume later)
```

## Commands

### tack abandon

Mark a run that must never go on.

```text
Abandon marks a run that must never go on, such as one whose old primary
was promoted again by hand: it records status.abandoned in the run's
ledger. tack resume then refuses the run, and a new run of the workload no
longer waits for it.

Abandon changes nothing else. A hold the run left on an object stays:
remove the holds once Git equals live, or start the next run with
--take-over RUN, which takes them over. Abandon lists the holds as the run's
ledger records them, and does not read the objects: a hold removed by hand
is still listed. The runbook:
  https://github.com/takodotid/tack/blob/main/docs/runbooks/abandon-run.md

kubectl get sfo shows when the run was abandoned in its Abandoned column,
from the CRD of tack v1.4.0 on. Its State column keeps the run's last
state, such as Stopped. tack status lists the run as Abandoned.

Abandon does not stop a tack process that runs the run: check first that
none does. A step that runs, such as a promotion, still changes the
workload. Abandon refuses while the run's lease says that a tack process
runs it, or, for a run without a lease, while the run is Running and its
ledger was written within its step's timeout and 5 minutes; --force goes on
all the same. Only tack v1.3.0 and later honour the mark: an older tack
resumes the run, and drops the mark.

An agent never starts "tack abandon" without an explicit instruction from a
human for that run.
```

Usage: `tack abandon RUN (-f WORKLOAD | --context CONTEXT) [flags]`

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `--context` | a kube context to read runs from, instead of --file |
| `-f`, `--file` | the Workload file: search every site of the workload for its runs |
| `--force` | go on although a tack process may still run the run: its lease is fresh, or its ledger was just written; only once a human checked that none does |
| `--namespace` | the ledger namespace, with --context (default `tack-system`) |
| `--reason` | why the run is abandoned, recorded in its ledger (default `abandoned by the operator with tack abandon`) |
| `--yes` | skip the confirmation; only when a human approved abandoning this run |

Examples:

```sh
  # After the old primary was promoted again by hand
  tack abandon pg-main-20261027-101500 -f workload.yaml --reason "a promoted again by hand"
```

### tack plan

Print every step and change of a run, and change nothing.

```text
Plan prints every step and every change a run would make, in order, and
changes nothing. It reads each reachable site and the Git repository.

The plan names the objects Tack holds, the patches and waits of the
workload's driver, the exact Git change (file, path and value), the checks
before and after release, and the follow-ups left for the operator. Values
captured during the run, such as a demotion token, appear as $(name).

Run "tack plan" before every "tack run", and show its output to the human
who decides.
```

Usage: `tack plan -f WORKLOAD --to SITE [--mode planned|site-loss] [flags]`

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `--accept-behind` | with --mode site-loss: promote the target although another site that answers holds WAL past it; that site then needs a rebuild |
| `-f`, `--file` | the Workload file (required) |
| `--from` | the site of the current primary (default: the primary the target site declares) |
| `--mode` | planned: the old primary demotes and hands over its token; site-loss: promote without a token after the old site is lost (default `planned`) |
| `-o`, `--output` | text, or yaml for the SiteFailover the run would create (default `text`) |
| `--to` | the site that becomes primary (required) |

Examples:

```sh
  # A planned switchover to site b
  tack plan -f workload.yaml --to b

  # A failover after site a is lost
  tack plan -f workload.yaml --to b --mode site-loss

  # The SiteFailover object the run would create
  tack plan -f workload.yaml --to b -o yaml
```

### tack prune

Delete the ledgers of old runs that succeeded.

```text
Prune deletes the ledgers of old runs of a workload, which every search
for runs reads at every site. It deletes only a run that succeeded with no
Deferred step, and keeps:

  - the newest --keep runs that succeeded, so the record of the last
    switch stays. Where the clocks of two sites' API servers cannot order
    two runs, created less than 10s apart, prune keeps both;
  - every abandoned run, and every run that another run took over: delete
    one by hand, as the link below shows;
  - every run with a follow-up Rebuild whose member was not created again
    10s or more after the run's ledger was created, as the Workload file
    names the member at that site, or whose member cannot be read: a
    planned run onto that member needs it;
  - every run whose spec records that it took over a run that is kept and
    is not marked as taken over: without it, that run would block new runs;
  - every run that a newer tack than this one last wrote.

Prune acts only when every site of the workload answers and every run of
the workload is finished: succeeded with no Deferred step, abandoned or
taken over. It lists each run with what it does to it, asks for the
workload's name, searches again, and stops, deleting nothing, when anything
changed. It then deletes the ledgers one at a time, by name, oldest first,
each only while it is the object that was read, and stops at the first
error. It changes nothing else.

Prune needs delete on sitefailovers in the ledger namespace at each site,
row 16 of the permissions table, and get on the workload's objects, row 7,
when a run has a follow-up Rebuild. The rules, and how to delete a kept
run by hand:
  https://github.com/takodotid/tack/blob/main/docs/operations.md#old-ledgers

An agent never starts "tack prune" without an explicit instruction from a
human for that workload.
```

Usage: `tack prune -f WORKLOAD --keep N [flags]`

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `-f`, `--file` | the Workload file: prune reads every site of the workload |
| `--keep` | keep this many of the newest runs that succeeded, 1 or more (default `0`) |
| `--yes` | skip the confirmation; only when a human approved deleting these ledgers |

Examples:

```sh
  # Keep the 20 newest runs that succeeded, and those prune must keep
  tack prune -f workload.yaml --keep 20
```

### tack resume

Continue a run from its ledger.

```text
Resume continues a run from its ledger, never from memory: it takes the
remaining steps of the plan the run recorded when it started, with the
workload configuration it recorded then.

A step that was started but not recorded as done runs again; every step is
safe to repeat. Steps deferred because their site was unreachable run once
the site is back.

When the run stopped, the ledger's reason says what the operator must
decide. Each decision has its own flag:

  GitChanged           Git moved during the run. Review the new commits, then
                       --accept-git-head SHA makes that commit the new base.
                       When the stop names one commit, SHA must be that
                       commit; HEAD stands for it.
  FencedSiteIsPrimary  a site other than the new primary answers as
                       primary. --fence SITE lets this resume demote it,
                       when the message offers it. A resume without it
                       stops again while the site is primary.
  HeldByAnotherRun     another run holds an object. Finish that run, or
                       start a new run with --take-over.

A run that another run took over, or that another run of the workload
started after, cannot resume: its plan may no longer match the sites. Nor
can a run whose steps are not the plan its driver composes from the
ledger's Workload: the ledger was written outside tack.

An agent never starts "tack resume" without an explicit instruction from a
human for that run, and never passes these flags unless that instruction
names them.
```

Usage: `tack resume RUN (-f WORKLOAD | --context CONTEXT) [flags]`

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `--accept-git-head` | accept this commit, pushed during the run, as the run's new Git base |
| `--context` | a kube context to read runs from, instead of --file |
| `--fence` | demote this site although it answers as primary (repeatable) |
| `-f`, `--file` | the Workload file: search every site of the workload for its runs |
| `--namespace` | the ledger namespace, with --context (default `tack-system`) |
| `--yes` | skip the confirmation; only when a human approved resuming this run |

Examples:

```sh
  # Continue after Git is back (break-glass)
  tack resume pg-main-20261027-101500 -f workload.yaml

  # Accept a commit pushed during the run, after reviewing it
  tack resume pg-main-20261027-101500 -f workload.yaml --accept-git-head 1a2b3c4

  # Demote a lost site that came back as primary
  tack resume pg-main-20261027-101500 -f workload.yaml --fence a
```

### tack run

Start a run: hold Flux, switch the primary, commit to Git, release.

```text
Run starts a run: it prints the plan, asks for confirmation, creates the
SiteFailover ledger at the target site, and takes the steps in order.

Modes:
  planned    the old primary demotes; its demotion token promotes the new one
  site-loss  the new site promotes without a token; the old primary then
             needs a rebuild. Use it only when the old site is lost:
             Tack cannot tell a lost site from one cut off from this
             host. Confirm the loss and fence the old primary first.

Confirming a site loss and fencing the old primary:
  https://github.com/takodotid/tack/blob/main/docs/runbooks/site-loss.md

If Git cannot be reached (break-glass), the run still holds and switches the
live objects, then waits with exit code 4; "tack resume" finishes it once Git
is back. If anything unexpected happens, the run stops with exit code 3 and
the ledger says why.

An agent never starts "tack run" without an explicit instruction from a
human for that run, and runs "tack plan" first.
```

Usage: `tack run -f WORKLOAD --to SITE [--mode planned|site-loss] [flags]`

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `--accept-behind` | with --mode site-loss: promote the target although another site that answers holds WAL past it; that site then needs a rebuild |
| `-f`, `--file` | the Workload file (required) |
| `--force` | with --take-over: go on although a tack process may still run the run: its lease is fresh, or its ledger was just written; only once a human checked that none does |
| `--from` | the site of the current primary (default: the primary the target site declares) |
| `--mode` | planned: the old primary demotes and hands over its token; site-loss: promote without a token after the old site is lost (default `planned`) |
| `--take-over` | take over the holds of this earlier run, which can no longer finish; repeat it for each run |
| `--to` | the site that becomes primary (required) |
| `--yes` | skip the confirmation; only when a human approved this run |

Examples:

```sh
  # A planned switchover to site b, after reviewing "tack plan"
  tack run -f workload.yaml --to b

  # A failover after site a is lost
  tack run -f workload.yaml --to b --mode site-loss
```

### tack status

Show runs and their steps from the ledger.

```text
Status shows runs from their ledgers. Without RUN it lists the runs of the
workload at every reachable site. A site that gives no answer is named after
the list, with its error, and status exits with code 1: the list is
incomplete. With -o yaml, the names follow the ledgers as YAML comments.

With RUN it shows that run's phases and steps with their times and
attempts, held objects as the ledger records them, Git commits, captured
values (by name only), follow-ups, and the history of the run's state: who
started, stopped and resumed it, when, with which tack and flags.
```

Usage: `tack status [RUN] (-f WORKLOAD | --context CONTEXT) [flags]`

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `--context` | a kube context to read runs from, instead of --file |
| `-f`, `--file` | the Workload file: search every site of the workload for its runs |
| `--namespace` | the ledger namespace, with --context (default `tack-system`) |
| `-o`, `--output` | text or yaml (default `text`) |

Examples:

```sh
  # Every run of the workload
  tack status -f workload.yaml

  # One run, step by step
  tack status pg-main-20261027-101500 -f workload.yaml
```

## Global flags

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `--kubeconfig` | kubeconfig file holding every site's context (default: the standard kubeconfig loading rules) |
